WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.740
When is a "co-ed shower scene" not just a
"co-ed shower scene"?

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:09.749
"Starship Troopers" is way more than just
casual nudity, fascist formalwear, and giant

00:00:09.749 --> 00:00:11.169
alien bugs.

00:00:11.169 --> 00:00:14.190
This sci-fi satire was lightyears ahead of
its time.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.780
"Starship Troopers" goes to work right away,
shaping its viewers' brains into the ideal

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.730
mindset for a soldier.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:26.680
The opening scenes feature commercials from
a 23rd-century regime known as the United

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:28.660
Citizen Federation.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:34.289
The clips vary from information on the savagery
of the arachnid enemy to positive outcomes

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:36.159
for enlisting in the military.

00:00:36.159 --> 00:00:41.050
The not-so-subtle brainwashing leaves you
confused about your feelings towards Starship

00:00:41.050 --> 00:00:45.640
Troopers, and that's helped this cult classic
shine brighter over the years.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.829
The military propaganda videos are truly a
unique form of satire, one that becomes more

00:00:50.829 --> 00:00:53.269
transparent with each viewing.

00:00:53.269 --> 00:00:57.620
Director Paul Verhoeven claimed to have only
read a couple chapters of the source material

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:03.320
— Robert Heinlein's 1959 novel — before
retiring it to the dust bin.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:05.510
In an interview with Empire, he explained:

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.080
"It's a very right-wing book.

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.220
And with the movie we tried, and I think at
least partially succeeded, in commenting on

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:18.880
that at the same time…All the way through
we were fighting with the fascism, the ultra-militarism."

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.280
Rather than glorify the book's fascist elements,
Verhoeven pulled us into the muck with an

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:28.630
awareness of something being terribly wrong
with this totalitarian society and its military

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:29.730
obsession.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:34.880
Its satirical attributes are constructed so
well that it's easy to miss the film's intentions

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:39.660
as it presents a developed form of fascism
on a cosmic scale.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.110
These details even sailed right over the heads
of many critics who accused Verhoeven of being

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:46.070
a neo-Nazi himself.

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:50.370
They completely misunderstood his achievement
in taking fascist rhetoric and dressing it

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.090
up in satire as a warning.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:01:57.750
The opening segment of "Starship Troopers"
teases us with news footage of the battlefield.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:03.630
Our primary protagonist, Johnny Rico, is delivered
a major blow from his adversaries before the

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:09.670
intro sequence fades out to a more peaceful
time, before all the mayhem on the bugs' planet,

00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:10.670
Klendathu.

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.740
When we do return to the same scene later
on, we get a different view of this same battle

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:19.040
through the eyes of the soldiers, complete
with shots of the cameraman who captured the

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.280
carnage in the propaganda video we saw earlier.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:27.240
While troops are unloading magazines at their
enemy, the man holding the studio camera remains

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.350
in the thick of it, documenting the bloodshed
with a steady hand.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:36.410
There are numerous ways to die for the cause,
and the news cameraman goes out in a barrage

00:02:36.410 --> 00:02:37.580
of cinematic glory.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:43.470
In the "Starship Troopers" universe, Earth
in the 23rd century looks to be a lavish time

00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:48.230
to be alive, complete with sleek technology
and bright, shiny lights.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:53.560
The central plot follows Rico closely while
he reaches his decision to enlist in the military,

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:57.490
along with his girlfriend, Carmen, and his
best friend, Carl.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:01.900
From this narrative viewpoint, the people
of Earth look like they live a cozy life.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:06.270
In reality, we're seeing the lifestyle of
someone in a much higher tax bracket.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:11.650
The tax plans in this world aren't explained
to us, but noticing the little details makes

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:15.740
it clear that Rico's parents are left with
plenty of scratch.

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:21.930
Rico's home is a spacious house with towering,
crystal clear windows, perched atop a mountainside.

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.820
The future looks wonderful from the viewpoint
of the wealthy.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:29.660
Also, lending credence to Rico's financial
upbringing is his father's counter-argument

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:31.020
for enlisting.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.671
Rather than doing his part for society, Rico's
dad would rather him go on vacation with the

00:03:35.671 --> 00:03:40.300
family before beginning his first semester
at Harvard, neither of which are available

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:41.980
to those from meager means.

00:03:42.980 --> 00:03:47.300
"Starship Troopers" does such a fantastic
job of world-building that you won't even

00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.120
recognize it's happening.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.739
In the same way that new recruits are brainwashed
into operating the cogs of war, we're subjected

00:03:54.739 --> 00:03:59.530
to the same devices to help us realize the
sordid reality of this future.

00:03:59.530 --> 00:04:03.670
The propaganda commercials aren't presented
as though the characters are watching them

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:08.390
at the moment, but as if the filmmakers are
recruiting us to learn more about the world

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:09.900
they created.

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:15.450
And laced throughout the informative commercials
are details about the vastness of this universe.

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:20.560
When we aren't getting battle advice, we are
presented with inter-planetary diagrams and

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:21.989
Earth politics.

00:04:21.989 --> 00:04:26.830
The commercials have a slightly humorous characteristic
to them, but don't let that distract you from

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.440
seeing the bigger picture.

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:33.470
The small details in each quick segment expands
on the world of "Starship Troopers".

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:37.720
It's through these moments that we learn about
the location of the bug planet, foreshadowing

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:42.520
about smart bugs, Earth's war council decisions,
and more.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:47.360
It's here we get to see the fascist bureaucracy
in action, and we see a world where the media

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.470
is controlled by the state.

00:04:49.470 --> 00:04:54.150
We know creating a mental block when seeing
commercials is instinctual for most denizens

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:57.580
of the 21st century, but we recommend paying
attention to these ones.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:03.680
As it stands today, we're approaching an Earth
population of 8 billion souls.

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:08.250
The exponential growth will likely lead to
gigantic numbers by the time we hit the 23rd

00:05:08.250 --> 00:05:09.250
century.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:14.410
On a planet led by a reactionary military
government like in "Starship Troopers", you'd

00:05:14.410 --> 00:05:18.440
think officials would enact a type of control
for population growth.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:24.130
Turns out there is one succinct chunk of dialogue
that informs us of the bureaucratic path towards

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:26.130
parenthood in this future.

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:30.949
It's buried in a scene that every single viewer
remembers ... and not for its informative

00:05:30.949 --> 00:05:31.949
dialogue.

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:37.070
The communal shower scene in "Starship Troopers"
has so much casual nudity that it distracts

00:05:37.070 --> 00:05:41.360
from all the expository dialogue that serves
to expand the world.

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:46.550
One of the soldiers begins asking his squad
mates their reason for enlisting in the military.

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:50.860
Among the answers are career ambitions, college
funding, and civic duty.

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.700
However, there's one especially interesting
answer from a female soldier, and it involves

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:57.639
a desire to have children.

00:05:57.639 --> 00:06:03.240
That tells us that not only do you have to
register for a license in order to have kids,

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:07.870
but it's also not a sure thing and requires
proving yourself to society.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:10.750
It's something to think about while sitting
in bumper-to-bumper traffic.

00:06:11.750 --> 00:06:15.699
Rico is delivered a tough blow when he loses
a soldier while leading his squad through

00:06:15.699 --> 00:06:16.919
a training exercise.

00:06:16.919 --> 00:06:20.740
We could argue about how the man's helmet
wouldn't have done much to protect him from

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:24.630
a bullet to the face, but that's of little
consequence.

00:06:24.630 --> 00:06:29.270
It's what happens after Rico signs his letter
of resignation that's interesting.

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:33.560
After the incident, his parents call him and
he informs them he's coming home.

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:38.660
The call ends with daylight slowly being snuffed
out over his mom and dad before the signal

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:39.740
goes dead.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:44.720
The reasoning behind the signal loss is explained
so quickly that you might miss it — it comes

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:50.130
so fast because, evidently, news stations
have a magical ability to assemble a compelling

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:51.930
news package instantaneously.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:57.950
As soon as Rico grabs his bag to leave after
his phone call, troops begin rushing to the

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:03.139
command center to take in the news of a bug
meteor that's decimated Rico's hometown of

00:07:03.139 --> 00:07:04.310
Buenos Aires.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:08.789
The time between the phone call with his parents
and the news being reported is less than a

00:07:08.789 --> 00:07:09.789
minute.

00:07:09.789 --> 00:07:13.590
Not only that, but in this span of time, the
war council has gathered and decided Earth

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:17.710
is officially going to war with the arachnids
of Klendathu.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:22.630
In a population obsessed with military might,
political bureaucracy moves much quicker when

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:23.930
war is involved.

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:29.009
The reaction to the meteor strike on Buenos
Aires happens so fast that you have to wonder

00:07:29.009 --> 00:07:33.190
if the government knew it was coming and saw
it as a desirable outcome.

00:07:33.190 --> 00:07:37.727
There's no better way to whip your population
into a frenzy and march them into battle.

00:07:38.150 --> 00:07:42.460
In the United Citizen Federation, becoming
a citizen is paramount.

00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:46.600
Citizenship comes with a highly sought-after
level of respect in this society.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:51.560
It opens pathways financially in the same
way that it does in current times, but the

00:07:51.560 --> 00:07:56.340
future residents of Earth view it as the only
respectable way to become a contributing member

00:07:56.340 --> 00:07:57.470
of society.

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:02.069
The most definitive statement in regards to
attaining value in this world comes from the

00:08:02.069 --> 00:08:05.800
funeral for Dizzy, when Rico is giving his
eulogy.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:10.280
The heartfelt speech is filled with emotion,
and it's also a declaration on what's required

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:11.729
to garner respect.

00:08:11.729 --> 00:08:14.160
It isn't enough to be a good friend.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.210
Being a soldier is admirable, but it's more
about the path it creates — a path towards

00:08:19.210 --> 00:08:24.080
the ultimate goal of upgrading from simply
being a civilian to being a citizen in the

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.410
Federation.

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:30.580
Individuality is of little importance, and
your life is only given value when offered

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.760
up as a sacrificial lamb for the cause.

00:08:33.760 --> 00:08:38.479
Anyone with basic understanding of human anatomy
will probably have trouble accepting the Brain

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:43.149
Bug's ability to absorb our knowledge by sucking
our gray matter out.

00:08:43.149 --> 00:08:47.500
But it's a little easier to accept this detail
when you remember the presence of psychics

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:49.320
in "Starship Troopers".

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:54.029
This ability isn't featured heavily, and it's
only talked about in scenes with Neil Patrick

00:08:54.029 --> 00:08:55.630
Harris' character, Carl.

00:08:55.630 --> 00:09:00.070
In an earlier scene, it looks like he's trying
to test Rico's psychic abilities with a playing

00:09:00.070 --> 00:09:05.589
card prediction game, but it's actually about
Carl flexing his own mental aptitude.

00:09:05.589 --> 00:09:10.920
During the psychic test, Carl is trying to
send a telepathic message to Rico, giving

00:09:10.920 --> 00:09:13.790
him the answer as to what card is being displayed.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:17.180
You can tell as much by Carl's mannerisms
during the test.

00:09:17.180 --> 00:09:21.600
The failed use of this skill plays a part
in showing us character growth in the latter

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.480
half of "Starship Troopers".

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:28.390
After he rescues Carmen, Rico posits that
it was Carl who mentally projected her location

00:09:28.390 --> 00:09:29.480
to him.

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:34.010
Carl slyly rejects the claim, but his smirk
reveals the truth about that statement.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:40.180
If the uniforms and symbols featured on the
United Citizen Federation's attire look suspiciously

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:42.970
Third Reich, it's because they are.

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:47.950
The filmmakers recognized the original "Starship
Troopers" novel as having an intriguing sci-fi

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:53.580
story, but they also acknowledged the book
contained a hearty dose of totalitarian rhetoric.

00:09:53.580 --> 00:09:58.990
Paul Verhoeven aimed to have that characteristic
shine through sarcastically in the form of

00:09:58.990 --> 00:09:59.990
satire.

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:04.779
The Nazi symbolism was meant to help you realize
that these characters may be heroes, but they're

00:10:04.779 --> 00:10:07.510
heroes in a fascist society.

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:11.870
By the end of the film, the propaganda commercials
have done a sufficient job of putting us into

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.060
the mindset of people living on Earth in the
23rd century.

00:10:15.060 --> 00:10:21.980
In order to pull back on the reins, Verhoeven
tried to make the nods to Nazi Germany obvious.

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:24.501
He stated in an interview with The Guardian:

00:10:24.501 --> 00:10:27.290
"These soldiers were like something out of
Nazi propaganda.

00:10:27.290 --> 00:10:29.690
I even put one in an SS uniform.

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:30.690
But no one noticed.

00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:35.950
I thought Neil Patrick Harris arriving on
the set in an SS uniform might clear it up."

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:40.810
The small detail didn't stop some critics
from warping the director's vision, instead

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:45.450
viewing "Starship Troopers" as a twisted nod
to the Third Reich.

00:10:45.450 --> 00:10:50.720
Verhoeven's motives may have been misunderstood
when the movie was released, but we see the

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.089
underlying intentions now.

00:10:53.089 --> 00:10:56.279
And we're still unsure about whether or not
we should laugh.

